{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Basic","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalGraphRealization"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.not_subLemma_alwaysPoleOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.not_subLemma_alwaysPoleOrder","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L260-L269","doc":"**A concrete non-model (PROVED).**  `alwaysPoleOrder` fails the\nsub-lemma already at axial distance `ρ = 0`, where the audit's Step 2\nindicator is `false`.  So the class is not satisfied by every\nassignment, which is what `combinatorialPoleOrder_subLemma` alone\ncould not establish. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.alwaysPoleOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.alwaysPoleOrder","kind":"def","line":255,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L255-L258","doc":"The \"every generator has a pole\" assignment — the shape a reader\nmight expect from the boxed identity read carelessly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.not_subLemma_of_poleOrder_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.not_subLemma_of_poleOrder_ne","kind":"theorem","line":243,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L243-L253","doc":"**…and it fails at any assignment that disagrees at one axial\ndistance (PROVED).**  This is the content the parameterisation buys:\nfor a *specific* `P` coming from the actual Hoefsmit matrix entries,\nthe sub-lemma is a real constraint that can fail. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.combinatorialPoleOrder_subLemma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.combinatorialPoleOrder_subLemma","kind":"instance","line":213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L213-L241","doc":"**The sub-lemma is satisfiable, at the indicator itself (PROVED).**\nSo an instance `[CrystalRMatrixSubLemma R q P]` for an *unspecified*\n`P` asserts nothing.  Any downstream result must say which `P`.\n\n**History (2026-08-24, bean `qou-09mr`).**  This replaces an\n`instance canonical` whose class field `poleIndicator` was set to\n`fun ρ => decide (ρ ≤ -1)` — the very function\n`pole_indicator_correct` compares against — so the propositional\nfield closed by `rfl`.  Its docstring conceded the point (\"the\nindicator IS the witness function\") and offered \"no `sorry`\" as\nreassurance; no `sorry` was exactly the problem, because nothing was\nleft visible to `#print axioms`.  It was also an `instance`, pinned\nby `let C := CrystalGraphRealization.canonical R q` to the one-point\ncrystal — and since that `def` is the corpus's only realisation of\n`CrystalGraphRealization`, every way of instantiating this class's\ntheorems at a concrete `R`, `q` got the free witness by resolution.\n\nNote what this witness does *not* depend on: `R`, `q`, and the\nrealisation `C` are free.  A consistency witness that holds for\nevery crystal-graph realisation, including\n`CrystalGraphRealization.canonical`'s one-point crystal, says\nnothing about any of them — it is a fact about the indicator\nfunction alone.  The old instance hid that by pinning itself to\n`CrystalGraphRealization.canonical R q`, which read as though a\ncrystal had been consulted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.combinatorialPoleOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.combinatorialPoleOrder","kind":"def","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L209-L211","doc":"The pole-order assignment that *is* the Step 2 indicator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.inter_nucleon_interface_pole","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.inter_nucleon_interface_pole","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L183-L197","doc":"Per-nucleon-shift additive linearity (recurrence, derived).\n\nThe atomic-braid Markov-trace shift exponent\n`shift(Z, N) = 18Z + 21N + 1`\n(per `prop:per-nucleon-shift-additive-linearity` already on\nmain) is recovered by the audit's Step 4 cycle composition: the\ninter-nucleon interface contributes `1` (the `T_{(2, 1)}` pole\nin `column_syt_pole_per_cycle 2 = 1`), each new proton contributes\n`18`, each new neutron contributes `21`.  Here we prove only the\ninterface piece — the per-nucleon `18` and `21` are inputs from\nthe AXKIA per-new-nucleon decomposition (cited in audit Step 5,\nnot provable from `pole_indicator_correct` alone). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.axkia_pole_product","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.axkia_pole_product","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L173-L181","doc":"**AXKIA multiplicative chain (boxed corollary).**\n\nThe combinatorial reading of `α_EM⁻¹ = q · [9]_q · [10]_q`:\nthe integer envelopes are `α_shift(T_{(2, 9)}) = 9` and\n`α_shift(T_{(3, 5)}) = 10`.  Their **product** is `90` —\nmatches `[9]·[10]` in the `q → 1` limit of `α_EM⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.cycle_pole_torus_knot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.cycle_pole_torus_knot","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L156-L171","doc":"**Cycle composition (audit Step 4).**\n\nFor `p ≥ 1` strands and `k` cycles, the column-SYT pole order\nof the torus-knot interface `T_{(p, k)} = (σ_1 σ_2 ⋯ σ_{p-1})^k`\nis `k * (p - 1)`.  Combinatorial proof: each of the `p - 1`\nboundary generators contributes a single pole (audit Step 2,\nvia `pole_indicator_correct` at axial distance `-1`); cycles\ncompose multiplicatively in `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.axkia_pole_T_3_5","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.axkia_pole_T_3_5","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L151-L154","doc":"Sanity: AXKIA force-carrier T_{(3, 5)} pole = 5·2 = 10\n(combinatorially: 5 cycles × 2 poles per cycle). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.axkia_pole_T_2_9","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.axkia_pole_T_2_9","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L146-L149","doc":"Sanity: AXKIA force-carrier T_{(2, 9)} pole = 9·1 = 9\n(combinatorially: 9 cycles × 1 pole per cycle). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.column_syt_pole_per_cycle_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.column_syt_pole_per_cycle_two","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L141-L144","doc":"Sanity: column-SYT pole-per-cycle at `p = 2` is 1 (canonical\npppnnn averaged interface). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.column_syt_pole_per_cycle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.column_syt_pole_per_cycle","kind":"def","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L129-L139","doc":"Pole-per-cycle on the column SYT of shape `(1^p)`.\n\nBy definition of a column, every adjacent pair `(j, j+1)` for\n`j = 1, …, p - 1` has axial distance `ρ_T(j) = -1` (cells\nstacked vertically; col fixed, row decreases by 1).  By\n`pole_indicator_correct`, every generator contributes a pole\nof order 1, so the cycle pole is `p - 1`.  Matches\n`prop:alpha-shift-torus-knot` (`α_shift(T_{(p, 1)}) = p - 1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.poleIndicator_plus_one_eq_false","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.poleIndicator_plus_one_eq_false","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L118-L127","doc":"Unconditional structural lemma: `poleIndicator (+1) = false`.\nThe pole order at the row-extreme axial distance is 0,\nmatching the eigenvalue `+q` of `σ_i` on a same-row SYT pair. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.poleIndicator_minus_one_eq_true","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.poleIndicator_minus_one_eq_true","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L106-L116","doc":"Unconditional structural lemma: `poleIndicator (-1) = true`.\nThe pole order at the column-extreme axial distance is 1,\nmatching the eigenvalue `-q⁻¹` of `σ_i` on a same-column SYT\npair (Hoefsmit; audit Step 1). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.pole_indicator_correct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.pole_indicator_correct","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L101-L101","doc":"**The proposition itself** (Step 2 of the audit): the supplied\npole order is exactly the indicator of `ρ ≤ -1`.  This is the\nfield a concrete model must discharge (not a `sorry` — the\ncontent is combinatorial, derived from the crystal R-matrix\naction at `q = 0`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.mk","kind":"ctor","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L94-L94","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.CrystalRMatrixSubLemma","kind":"class","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L73-L102","doc":"**Crystal R-matrix Sub-lemma 2.1 (proposition, conditional), as a\npredicate ON a given pole-order assignment.**\n\nConditional on\n[`conj:crystal-graph-realization`](crystal-graph-realization.md),\nthe matrix-entry pole order of `A^λ_{μ, ν}(σ_i)` at `q = 0`\non any SYT `T` in the `(μ, ν)`-block is\n\n  pole_order(σ_i, T) = 1   iff   ρ_T(i) ≤ -1\n                     = 0   otherwise\n\nwhere `ρ_T(i) = c(cell of i+1 in T) - c(cell of i in T)` is\nthe axial distance and `c(cell) = col - row`.\n\nThe single field asserts that identity **about the supplied `P`**;\nthe class can no longer manufacture its own indicator. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:crystal-r-matrix-sub-lemma","label":"prop:crystal-r-matrix-sub-lemma","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/crystal-r-matrix-sub-lemma.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PoleOrderData.poleOrder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PoleOrderData.poleOrder","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L71-L71","doc":"Pole order at `q = 0` as a function of the axial distance `ρ`:\n`true` for a pole of order 1, `false` for order 0. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PoleOrderData.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PoleOrderData.mk","kind":"ctor","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L68-L68","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.PoleOrderData","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.PoleOrderData","kind":"structure","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/CrystalRMatrixSubLemma.lean#L52-L71","doc":"**Matrix-entry pole orders as *given* data.**\n\n`poleOrder ρ` is the pole order at `q = 0` of the matrix entry of\n`A^λ_{μ, ν}(σ_i)` on an SYT whose axial distance at `i` is `ρ`,\nrecorded as a `Bool` (`true ↦ pole of order 1`, `false ↦ order 0`;\nStep 1 of the audit rules out higher orders).\n\n**Why a structure and not a class field (2026-08-24, bean\n`qou-09mr`).**  When the pole orders are a *field of the class*, an\ninstance gets to choose the very function the propositional field\ncompares it against, and the choice `fun ρ => decide (ρ ≤ -1)`\nsatisfies the comparison by `rfl`.  That is how the previous\nencoding was discharged for free — no `sorry`, and nothing\nestablished.  Carried as data and taken as a class *parameter*\nbelow, the assignment must be named by whoever assumes something\nabout it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}